00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:12.523
A market buy for 200 shares of a TSX listed name at the open, on a morning when the index

00:12:12.523 --> 00:12:17.823
is already down more than 250 points, is where the displayed quote stops being the price

00:12:17.823 --> 00:12:18.243
you get.

00:12:18.743 --> 00:12:23.623
The bid-ask spread can jump from 3 cents to 18 cents within the first two minutes because

00:12:23.623 --> 00:12:29.103
market makers pull quotes or widen them to protect against adverse selection when order-flow spikes

00:12:29.103 --> 00:12:30.663
and inventory risk rises.

00:12:30.663 --> 00:12:35.623
prices. So the fill lands several cents worse than the last printed price even when the underlying

00:12:35.623 --> 00:12:38.823
index move is only 0.8%.

00:12:38.823 --> 00:12:43.643
Liquidity providers also cut posted size at the best bid and offer on those sessions,

00:12:44.043 --> 00:12:48.763
which is why investors who fire market orders in the first minutes often see effective prices

00:12:48.763 --> 00:12:51.643
15 to 25 cents away from the previous close.

00:12:52.323 --> 00:12:57.903
The spread just went from 3 cents to 18 cents and your size is 200 shares. Do you hit market

00:12:57.903 --> 00:12:59.543
or sit on a limit at the mid?

00:12:59.543 --> 00:13:05.123
Enter as a limit at the midpoint of the current spread, refresh it every 30 seconds rather

00:13:05.123 --> 00:13:09.623
than accepting the first market fill, and on high volume days, that habit can save

00:13:09.623 --> 00:13:11.923
10 to 20 basis points per round turn.

00:13:12.583 --> 00:13:16.603
The misconception is that a market order always executes at the displayed quote.

00:13:17.183 --> 00:13:22.043
The fix is to read the level 2 ladder, size the limit no larger than the visible depth

00:13:22.043 --> 00:13:26.543
at your target price, and check whether large sellers are stacked at the offer before

00:13:26.543 --> 00:13:27.043
you submit.

